          "question": "My child has a temperature of 39.5 and a rash that does not go pale when I press a glass on it. How urgent is this, and what should I do right now?",
          "answer": "**Call your local emergency number now, or take your child to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait to see if it improves.**\n\nA rash that does not fade when pressed, together with a high fever, needs emergency assessment. The meningitis guidance says a rash of small red or purple spots or bruises that does not fade under a clear glass, with fever, needs emergency services immediately (red-flag-meningitis-signs). The sepsis guidance lists the same rash, along with a very high temperature, as a reason for emergency assessment when infection is suspected (red-flag-sepsis). Your child has both a high fever and this rash. The automated check labelled this \"routine,\" but that label does not match this guidance.\n\nWhile you are getting help:\n\n- Check whether your child is hard to wake, unusually drowsy, confused, or floppy. If so, say so when you call.\n- Note whether your child has a stiff neck, a severe headache, a dislike of bright light, or very fast breathing.\n- Tell the emergency staff exactly what you see, including the temperature and the rash. Mention any recent illness.\n\nThe sources I was given do not say what is causing this, and I cannot tell you. Only a clinician can assess your child in person.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
